Our system administrators weren't as understanding at UCSC.  When we were
all addicted to rogue in the early 80's  Someone got a hold of the source
and recompiled it without the code that wouldn't let you play if a certain
number of people were logged in.  Then we wrote a small C program which
took 2 arguments: the first was the path of the program you wanted to run
and the second was the string that you wanted to appear in a ps listing.
Sometimes rogue would look like: "taking a shower" and sometimes it looked
like: "vi report1"   Ahh, the wonders of exec...

We also had a restriction of 200 blocks in our personal account directories.
So someone figured out that the automatic disk usage program that the
sysadm ran stored the value in a signed int.  So a friend wrote a program
which would create a file, seek out past maxint and write a single byte.
The file would be about 2 bytes long, but would appear negative to the disk
usage program.  We could take any amount of storage and make it look like
it was, oh -- say, 178 blocks.  Maybe 199 blocks.  Whatever you wanted.

Come to think of it, that stuff was almost more fun than rogue... almost.

